tests.numbers:
Answer to ask 7: operations backs the 10-year.
10-year:
- EIS 2055 on a 2045 launch;
- stress net debt held at $0.0B in all four 10-year rows;
- peak cash about $8.44B (14% of $60.32B), in about 2051;
- strain with a same-round Re-engine: 2 years, $1.2B, legal.
7-year:
- EIS 2052;
- about $9.29B a year on average, 101% of stress FCF ($9.19B);
- stress net debt $0.5B at Renton alone, up to $4.7B;
- strain with the Re-engine: 5 years, barred.
The 10-year leads by $1.21-1.53B in every column and has the higher worst column (-2.94 at Renton against -4.15). The 7-year's 3-year lead buys 3 points of long-run split (35/65 against 32/68), and the grid already nets that.

Do Nothing:
- no programme cash;
- base FCF $13.79B (2045), $13.27B (2050), $12.76B (2055), $12.66B (2060); stress about $9.19B before erosion;
- net debt $0.0B in base and stress to 2050;
- profit against the game baseline -$0.66B (2050), -$1.30B (2055), -$1.43B (2060).
Re-engine:
- $5B over 2045-50, about $1.0B a year: 7.3% of base and 10.9% of stress FCF;
- net debt held at $0.0B in every column;
- undiscounted penalty $0.6B, for scale only (the grid already nets it).
Return: 0.73 - 1.54p at odds p of an A350 Re-engine. That is +0.73 at 0, +0.27 at 30%, +0.11 at 40%, with break-even at 47%. Thin conversion, inside ε at any odds.

other_moves.detail:
The case: a 10-year ramp at Renton, with Pope's signed, conditional MAX wind-down. Her three conditions:
- the ramp triggers the wind-down, not a date;
- no airplane is built at rate before certification [BX-1263];
- Renton is chosen at least 3 years before EIS.

Engines: Code 7 (CFM + the P&W-RR Joint Venture, two sources) under test. One lead installation is certified for EIS and the second follows on, so certification never runs concurrent with production. Code 3 (CFM alone) is the fallback if a durability problem is reported on the Joint Venture engine.

Terms: cost-plus or risk-share, never fixed price [BX-1262, BX-1304].

The package:
- the row against Do Nothing in every column, with the buffer test;
- the erosion to 2060;
- the trend since 2030, in both 2026 PV and decision-year dollars;
- the bill against base and stress FCF;
- the stress net-debt peak;
- the full cost of a +3-year slip, taken once;
- every premium.

The case will be judged on the 2050 grid alone. Malave projects -1.08 to -1.28 against Do Nothing. If that is what the grid shows, we will say so rather than launch to avoid saying it. No date [B-2315].

Durability first [BX-1298]. It comes back in 2050 only if three things hold:
- no A350 Re-engine is on the public record (H3, second half);
- it is H4-legal against any 2050 fps: 2 years against a 2050 10-year, with no slack;
- the grid gives it a real case.
That 2050 case is about +0.44 at best, below the Board's bar.

decision.best_grid_plan_in_expected_scenario:
The best plan is fps: hold + 787 Re-engine, at -0.75 against Do Nothing's -0.95 (+0.19 at 35% odds; about +0.01 after Malave's haircut). That is inside ε. The tie goes to Do Nothing on four counts:
- doing less [BX-1239];
- the higher-confidence plan [BX-0548];
- the higher worst column (-1.16 against -1.97);
- no added penalty.
The Board's Business case test would veto the Re-engine anyway.

The best fps row, 10-year at Renton + Re-engine, scores -2.78 expected, which is -1.83 against Do Nothing. The 10-year at Renton alone is -1.78.

