Impact on end-2045 net cash at the 48% practice:
- without the Re-engine: from $50.5B to $1.1B ($50.5B - $1.0B - $48.32B);
- with the Re-engine: $0.1B.

Item values for deletion:
- one move against none: -11.46/+0.06/+0.06/+0.44/+0.06/-11.46/+0.05;
- one move with the Re-engine, against the Re-engine alone: -11.46/+0.06/+0.06/+0.43/+0.06/-11.46/+0.06;
- it never reaches $1B.

This is a red line, binding. Bottleneck I could not defend to the Audit Committee.

(1) Default (ngsa hold, rea350 hold, delay_tactics none, a320_plan alt): passes every test.

(2) Re-engine row passes:
- peak cash ($1.0B, $4.2B of headroom);
- rule 6 (zero overlap);
- the teams.md row test (worst S7 +13.33);
- the -$2B line;
- rule 3 (open today);
- net cash.
It fails the added-move robustness test (-0.50 / -0.51 in S6 / S7, at most +0.89). That is a binding soft veto, not a red line, and not overridable at a margin below $1B.

(3) Every Delay Tactics row: red lines under rules 4 and 5 (both also fails the second-move rule), integrity for bottleneck, and the -$2B line. Deleted.

No quality escape is reported.
- Watch item: the last 28 A320s, built on the fewest lines with certified staff kept to the last aircraft. No line staff move to NGSA or A350 work before the last delivery.
- Statement flags:
  - windows, not dates;
  - no A320 closure year in public;
  - no engine named for any A350 development, and no Re-engine signal unless the Board approves one;
  - no Delay Tactics.
- NGSA's two engines (CFM, and the P&W-Rolls-Royce Joint Venture) are mature and in service, so they can be named.
The CEO's draft public line passes on these terms.

No crunch is reported for engines, structures, titanium or rare earths.
- Engine load, at two engines per aircraft:
  - 2044: 2,770 (368 A320neo + 2,402 NGSA), carried with no crunch;
  - 2045: 2,668 (56 + 2,612);
  - 2047-50: 2,678 (NGSA only);
  - from 2053: 2,732.
  Every later year sits below the 2044 peak, so the last step needs no new supply peak.
- NGSA sources: CFM plus the P&W-Rolls-Royce Joint Venture, so 2 sources from 3 makers.
The crunch trigger does not fire, and it does not block an A350 Re-engine. The missing widebody engine is a readiness problem, not a crunch (see the engine test).

NGSA was developed in 2030-37; the Re-engine would be developed in 2045-50 (EIS 2050). The overlap is 0 years, so the strain is $3B × 0 / 5 = $0. It would be the only launch this round.
The loads running alongside are not development:
- NGSA in-service support;
- NGSA's last steps (+33 a year, then +27);
- the A320 closure, which frees line staff from 2047.
Recruitment stays sized to our own needs, so the A320 sustaining teams and the A350 teams are not stripped. The test passes, so there is nothing to bind.

other_moves.detail:
We re-test only on these conditions:
- the case has its own grid item, which must show at least $1B over Do Nothing and must not turn negative in any plausible column;
- no 787 Re-engine is on the record at submission (rule 3);
- a widebody engine is on the record that is mature by the Re-engine's EIS, ideally committed a year before our launch (profile section 11.3);
- A350F and Spirit A350 absorption is shown by a milestone;
- Toepfer's and Wagner's tests both pass.
Alongside this, Wagner holds a non-public technical dialogue with Rolls-Royce on standard terms about Trent XWB durability and widebody engine maturity. It makes no commitment and gives no price, no date and no public Re-engine signal. Any announcement is a capital-markets matter for the Board [AX-0004].

memo_weighing.cfo:
Toepfer's tests are role-derived (Low confidence).

Passed:
- peak cash on both rows (Re-engine $1.0B a year, $4.2B of headroom; default nil);
- rule 6 (zero overlap);
- the row robustness test (default worst column S4 +13.29; Re-engine worst S7 +13.33);
- the -$2B line for the Re-engine (-0.51);
- net cash, payout and buyback on his method;
- the capex-timing and fewer-Board-items tie-breaks, both for hold.

Failed, with his veto binding:
- A350 Re-engine, on the added-move robustness test (+0.89 x5 / -0.50 / -0.51; no $1B). This is a soft veto, not a red line. I accepted it: no override is possible at +0.89, and he declined to argue against the within-$1B rule. He discloses that the failure rests on the added-move reading, not the teams.md row test. I rest the decision on three independent grounds, so his reading is not decisive alone.
- Any Delay Tactics (rules 4 and 5, the fine and the second move). These are red lines; deleted.

I took his recommended orders in full and his net-cash path for the Audit Committee paper. I took his cancel arithmetic as moot, since nothing was launched.

memo_weighing.ops:
Wagner's tests are role-derived (Very low confidence).

Passed on both rows:
- safety, quality, the A320 ramp shield and supply crunch (the four the Board never lets me override);
- production readiness for NGSA's last step and the A320 closure;
- engineering capacity and rule 6 (zero overlap);
- airframe readiness on the A350 lines.

A350F and Spirit absorption passed on a steady-rate milestone: 70 a year from 2026 to 2044 with no event. I accept it with his stated limit: no dated A350F EIS or Spirit close-out milestone exists in the sources.

Failed, with his veto binding:
- A350 Re-engine, on engine readiness. No A350 widebody engine is launched or committed. UltraFan widebody would be ready in 2051 at the earliest, and in 2056 on Rolls-Royce's sequence. The GEnx upgrade is a 787 engine. Doctrine's sequencing (section 11.3) is not met. This is a soft veto. I accepted it: he cites a concrete readiness constraint, the margin cannot carry an override, and on the five pillars an EIS ahead of a mature engine is not something I would take to the Board in any case.
- Any Delay Tactics (rule 4, integrity). A red line; deleted.

I adopted:
- his statement flags (no A350 engine named, no Re-engine signal, no A320 closure year);
- his sizing of supply and staffing for the ECSC;
- his safety-review items, including the A350 single-source watch;
- his non-public, standard-terms engine dialogue with Rolls-Royce as preparation for 2050.

