frame.situation:
PUBLIC RECORD (round 3 bulletin)
- NGSA (Airbus): launched 2030, in service since 2037. Engine code 7, fitted CFM + P&W + RR. Airbus runs the Alt plan. It says NGSA certifies "on the clock of maturity" and that the A350 "remains our widebody focus".
- Joint Venture (P&W and RR): formed 2035 when both sides were committed. Both Solos folded in (P&W's GTF2 and our UltraFan NB). Engine ready since 2036.
- P&W's published terms:
  - risk, investment and revenue are shared;
  - its gear and core stay P&W's work-share and intellectual property;
  - what each side brings counts only as a valuation in the share split, with no licence and no cash;
  - partner content enters only after EIS, as an engineering change with its own test evidence.
  These terms credit what each side brings, which was our condition [RX-0072].
- I record a miss plainly. In 2035 I put P&W not committing at 0.90. It committed. The fold saved $2.29B of our own money.
- CFM/GE:
  - ducted engine launched 2035, ready 2041;
  - Embraer partnership launched 2035, ready 2042 (open, not exclusive);
  - GEnx upgrade ready since 2033, which took our widebody share from 58.0% to 55.2% from 2042;
  - RISE stays a technology programme.
- Boeing:
  - no fps, no date and no fixed-price bet: "The market case for us isn't there yet";
  - the MAX holds at 57 a month;
  - it is improving today's 787 with GE, which in its own words is "not a 787 Re-engine order".
- No 787 Re-engine and no A350 Re-engine is launched or announced.
- Observed 2044 deliveries:
  - narrowbody engines CFM 54.1% / P&W 24.7% / RR 21.2%;
  - widebody engines CFM/GE 44.8% / RR 55.2%;
  - Boeing's narrowbody share of deliveries is down to 33.1%.

OUR POSITION (brief §1)
- On hold, ΔPV is +11.03: engine value +16.74, less R&D of 4.00 and write-offs of 1.71.
- The 2035 grid showed +11.77 in S1. The fall comes from CFM's ducted engine and the Embraer partnership entering the record, together with the equal-share rule (inference).
- Our narrowbody share: 8.5% (2040), 23.6% (2045), 26.4% (2050), 30.7% (2060).
- Our widebody share: 55.2% from 2042 to 2060.

OBJECTIVE STATUS (brief §2)
- Enter narrowbody: MET (23.6% in 2045, 26.4% in 2050).
- Widebody dominance: MET (worst year 55.2%, 5.2 points above the line, in every column and every plan).
- Bounded narrowbody risk (≤ $4B): NOT MET. We are at $5.71B ($4.00B Joint Venture share plus $1.71B fold write-off). It is beyond repair.
- Widebody never starved: MET ($0 strain), but only while both widebody items stay at hold.

COLUMNS
- S1: rivals hold.
- S2: Boeing launches a 10-year fps and selects us on code 5. It is −0.04 against S1 on the hold row, so a Boeing selection adds nothing on this grid.
- S3: 787 Re-engine.
- S4: 787 and A350 Re-engines.

BOARD GUIDANCE (non-binding; its veto comes after my decision)
- Risk appetite 4 of 5. It calls this a delivery round and expects every field at hold, premium_b 0 and no balance-sheet trigger.
- Would approve: all four fields at hold, plus these other moves:
  - Trent durability inside the existing budget;
  - UltraFan technology pull-through inside a broadly flat R&D envelope;
  - a Joint Venture support and capacity plan;
  - standard terms through the Investment Committee;
  - the Joint Venture engine on offer to any new single-aisle, with no dates and no concessions.
